================================================================================================================
(4) ★ THE SIGN OF LAMBDA -- newly observable, and paper 3 has it backwards
================================================================================================================
  Every ratio above needed a MINUS sign to come out near +1.  Under this programme's
  script convention  lambda = (1/4)log(r(n-2)/r(n+2)),  lambda < 0 below the centre,
  and the measured response has the opposite sign to Phi^{(q)}'(0).  The convention
  that matches is
        lambda(n) = +d/dm log r|_{m=n} = (1/4) log( r(n+2) / r(n-2) ),
  which is POSITIVE below the centre -- and that is the sign that makes paper 3's
  rem:tiltslope ratio s/lambda positive, with s > 0 for rho < 1/2.
  paper 3 def:slope writes lambda = -d/dm log r = (1/4)log(r(n-2)/r(n+2)); the two
  halves of that line disagree in sign, and rem:tiltslope uses the other one.
  It has never mattered: Phi is EVEN in lambda, so the sign was unobservable.
  Splitting the cosh makes it observable.  ERRATUM for paper 3, and paper 4 must state
  the convention before Phi^{(q)} is written down.

